{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Turn not to their meal-offering.</b> It is known that the burnt-offering and the meal-offering bring extension of wrath even upon the wicked, and therefore he prayed that His wrath not be extended for them but that He punish them immediately. And Rashi, of blessed memory, explained 'turn not to their meal-offering' — that is the incense that they will offer before You tomorrow, do not turn to it; it appears that his opinion is that 'their meal-offering' — that of Dathan and Abiram — will return (be rejected), and so too is the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>And if</b> so, one should ask: why did Moses pray only about them, for they were not the principal ones; and further, he knew of no merit on their behalf that would stand to protect them that he should be concerned; and further, he should have prayed about all of them collectively, that the punishment fall equally upon all their heads — so why did he single out these alone in his prayer? Therefore it appears to me to say that 'their meal-offering' refers back to the princes, regarding the offering that they brought at the dedication of the altar — each of them a bowl and a basin, both filled with fine flour mixed with oil for a meal-offering — and this is the meaning of מנחתם. And Moses was not concerned to pray about Dathan and Abiram, for they were not the principal ones and no merit was known for them; and further, from the moment they said 'we will not go up,' he recognized in them that their mouths had caused them to stumble, and they were already lost in his eyes. But he prayed about the princes, who were the principal ones among the two hundred and fifty, and among them as a group a great merit had occurred on the day of dedication, so he needed to pray that the merit of that offering not stand for them, and that they perish in their punishment. And after he prayed about the princes regarding the annulment of their merit, he turned back and defended himself concerning the words of Dathan and Abiram — לא חמור אחד מהם נשאתי, 'I have not taken a single donkey of theirs' — which is a reply to what they said: כי תשתרר עלינו גם השתרר, 'will you lord it over us?' And so on. And the opinion of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, is that Moses prayed that their portion not be accepted in the communal daily offerings (תמידי צבור), for every single one of Israel has a portion in them by means of the half-shekels." "<b>I have not taken a single donkey of theirs.</b> It is the way of rulers over a people who exercise authority over them to make use of the people therein, and even of their beasts, according to what they need for their work. And therefore Moses speaks in prayer and defends himself, that never did he conduct himself with this kind of dominion over Israel, to make use of a single donkey of theirs to put any load upon it — and not even with people, and yet they say that he lords it over them. And so we find with Samuel the prophet, peace be upon him, that he defended himself on this very matter and said (I Samuel 12:3): ״הנני ענו בי נגד ה׳ ונגד משיחו את שור מי לקחתי וחמור מי לקחתי את מי עשקתי את מי רצותי ומיד מי לקחתי כופר״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:11.758648422Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}